1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,600
The most sophisticated jumbo jet on the planet.

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:09,200
A helicopter hardened against electromagnetic pulses.

3
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:11,680
Armor-plated limousines.

4
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:15,080
These aren't your ordinary planes, trains, and automobiles.

5
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:16,640
These are top secret.

6
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:20,380
And there are only two ways to find out what they're really like inside.

7
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:23,340
Get elected or stay tuned.

8
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:27,180
Now, Presidential Movers on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
On September 11, 2001, during the chaos of the terrorist attacks, President George W.

10
00:00:47,478 --> 00:00:48,300
Bush

11
00:00:48,301 --> 00:00:52,820
boarded a special four-engine turbofan Boeing 747 jumbo jet.

12
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:56,400
Tail number 28,000.

13
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:59,320
Like any Air Force vehicle carrying the President,

14
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:02,360
this craft was designated Air Force One.

15
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:05,800
With the President a potential target,

16
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:09,920
the jet took off at an angle steeper than this more typical takeoff

17
00:01:09,921 --> 00:01:12,840
to avoid a possible surface-to-air missile assault.

18
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:16,260
The passengers on board thought it was near vertical ascent out,

19
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:19,420
full throttle, you know, in a national emergency.

20
00:01:19,421 --> 00:01:23,200
And, of course, as that fateful day unfolded,

21
00:01:23,201 --> 00:01:30,900
Air Force One was the extension of the White House, a refuge, a means of travel, a symbol of the American presidency.

22
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:37,420
This plane, one of two nearly identical jumbo jets designed for presidential use,

23
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:43,840
was deployed in a deception maneuver as Bush was taken from Florida on a circuitous route back to Washington.

24
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,080
Air Force One is not a stealth aircraft.

25
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:53,060
Still, had it come under attack, the pilots would have had at their disposal certain defensive measures.

26
00:01:53,200 --> 00:02:02,520
There is a passive anti-missile defense system in the tail of the aircraft that is designed to defeat infrared missiles.

27
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:10,640
By emitting a heat source that produces more heat than the exhaust of the aircraft engines,

28
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:18,940
a decoy system can fool the missile and draw the missile away from the aircraft.

29
00:02:18,941 --> 00:02:26,540
Civilian analysts also speculate that Air Force One is insulated against microchip-damaging

30
00:02:26,541 --> 00:02:33,180
EMPs, or electromagnetic pulses, like those produced by a nuclear blast.

31
00:02:33,181 --> 00:02:39,480
The purpose, of course, is for Air Force One to be able to fly in wartime, including nuclear

32
00:02:39,481 --> 00:02:42,540
wartime, and to be able to carry the president.

33
00:02:42,541 --> 00:02:48,420
Much of this is shrouded in secrecy, but we know such systems exist and they would represent

34
00:02:48,421 --> 00:02:53,780
the very state of art when it comes to providing defensive capabilities for an airplane.

35
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:59,540
Lay experts strongly believe that Air Force One does not have offensive weapons.

36
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:09,540
After all, it is in the end a transport airplane, and it is not a weapon of war,

37
00:03:09,541 --> 00:03:13,112
although it can be a wartime command post.

38
00:03:13,113 --> 00:03:17,620
Air Force One can fly at speeds approaching 630 miles

39
00:03:17,621 --> 00:03:27,300
an hour as high as 45,000 feet above the earth this 800,000 pound 231 foot long jet contains some

40
00:03:27,301 --> 00:03:32,340
of the most advanced communications equipment in the world including encryption and scrambling

41
00:03:32,341 --> 00:03:39,220
devices for secure links to the ground there are also 85 phones including secure lines and two-way

42
00:03:39,221 --> 00:03:45,860
radios plus fax machines 19 televisions and computer ports a conference room and private

43
00:03:45,861 --> 00:03:51,620
presidential suite, plus a galley capable of preparing 2,000 meals, keep the president a

44
00:03:51,621 --> 00:03:55,956
functioning chief executive miles above the Earth's surface.

45
00:03:55,957 --> 00:03:57,980
An operating room next to an

46
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:00,981
emergency room can handle medical crises.

47
00:04:00,982 --> 00:04:04,860
The plane has become an extension of the White House.

48
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:06,757
It's a flying Oval Office.

49
00:04:06,758 --> 00:04:11,180
Without Air Force One, getting the president back to the Capitol

50
00:04:11,181 --> 00:04:14,900
quickly and safely on September 11th would have been far more difficult.

51
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:21,500
All presidential movers are designed to keep the president safe,

52
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:23,980
and by extension, are government working.

53
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:30,840
For more than 150 years, trains, autos, airplanes, and helicopters

54
00:04:30,841 --> 00:04:34,040
have been transformed into high-tech cocoons.

55
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:39,620
We need to safely transport the most powerful person in the world,

56
00:04:39,621 --> 00:04:44,400
but these vehicles have become potent symbols themselves of the office they serve.

57
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:48,620
Nixon landing in China, opening up China.

58
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:50,200
What greater image do we have of that?

59
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:54,580
Of the American plane, you know, here's America, guys, you know,

60
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:59,540
and Reagan leaving the negotiating table, calling Gorbachev's bluff in Iceland

61
00:04:59,541 --> 00:05:01,440
and getting in his plane and flying back.

62
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:04,820
We have the world defined by a president who can get on a plane,

63
00:05:04,821 --> 00:05:11,860
fly across half the world, stand before a wall built by a dictatorial regime, and defy that regime.

64
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:17,740
And therefore, as a free man, I take pride in the word,

65
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:25,120
And they often play their part in our most profound national dramas.

66
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:28,880
Kennedy's assassination in 1963.

67
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:31,940
Roosevelt's death in 1945.

68
00:05:31,941 --> 00:05:35,940
Nixon's resignation in 1974.

69
00:05:35,941 --> 00:05:41,940
Perhaps the most technologically impressive of all the presidential movers are the airplanes.

70
00:05:41,941 --> 00:05:48,940
The current 747s are the latest in an evolution that began with the first airplane to ever take a president aloft,

71
00:05:48,941 --> 00:05:52,940
in St. Louis, Missouri, back in 1910.

72
00:05:52,941 --> 00:05:55,940
The first president to fly was Theodore Roosevelt.

73
00:05:55,941 --> 00:05:58,940
However, he was not the president of the United States at the time.

74
00:05:58,941 --> 00:06:02,600
left office, but he actually got into a Wright Brothers aircraft and went around.

75
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:09,320
This historic and dangerous trip, captured by another fledgling technology, motion pictures,

76
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:13,720
was the first of a series of presidential milestones to take place in the air.

77
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:23,300
Another Roosevelt was the first presidential candidate to fly during a run for office.

78
00:06:23,301 --> 00:06:34,160
In 1932, Franklin Delano Roosevelt flew on a Ford trimotor from Albany, New York, to Chicago to accept the nomination of the Democratic Party.

79
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:37,960
And this was really an extraordinary event.

80
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:45,800
We have a perfect day for this trip, and I'm very happy to be going out to Chicago, and everybody knows the reason why I'm so happy.

81
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,440
Breaking precedent, Roosevelt became the first candidate to accept his party's nomination in person.

82
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:57,880
Flying to the convention was as much a political as a practical consideration.

83
00:06:58,460 --> 00:07:03,700
Maybe he also saw the sense of modernity with flight, you know,

84
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:08,800
descending in Chicago as this redemptive figure

85
00:07:08,801 --> 00:07:12,680
who's going to lead the United States out of the dark days of the Great Depression.

86
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:19,240
Necessity rather than symbolism forced Roosevelt back on board during the Second World War.

87
00:07:19,241 --> 00:07:32,179
In January of 1943, a full 11 years after his trip to Chicago, he became the first sitting president to fly, traveling to a conference in Casablanca in a Boeing 314

88
00:07:32,180 --> 00:07:33,520
Clipper seaplane.

89
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:39,880
The journey took 90 hours in flying time round trip and covered over 17,000 miles.

90
00:07:39,881 --> 00:07:47,920
The secrecy was such that the crew of the Dixie Clipper only knew that a Mr. Jones,

91
00:07:47,921 --> 00:07:53,140
and who was passenger number one, was going to board the Dixie Clipper for the flight.

92
00:07:53,141 --> 00:08:00,040
But they were escorted by fighters and they made this arduous trip down to Brazil and then they

93
00:08:00,041 --> 00:08:08,080
crossed the South Atlantic to British Gambia and then he was transferred to a C-54 four-engine

94
00:08:08,081 --> 00:08:11,080
that flew into Casablanca.

95
00:08:11,081 --> 00:08:13,080
Roosevelt's appearance in Casablanca

96
00:08:13,081 --> 00:08:15,080
allowed him to engage in personal diplomacy

97
00:08:15,081 --> 00:08:17,080
with his Allied counterparts

98
00:08:17,081 --> 00:08:20,080
at a crucial stage in the war.

99
00:08:20,081 --> 00:08:22,080
In December of that same year,

100
00:08:22,081 --> 00:08:26,080
he flew to Tehran on a C-54 Skymaster.

101
00:08:26,081 --> 00:08:29,080
Clearly, the president needed a plane at his disposal,

102
00:08:29,081 --> 00:08:34,080
and a Skymaster was soon converted for his use.

103
00:08:34,081 --> 00:08:37,080
Now, this aircraft had special features.

104
00:08:37,081 --> 00:08:40,080
It has special features. It has an armor-proof window,

105
00:08:40,081 --> 00:08:43,080
so the president wants to look out the window, he certainly can.

106
00:08:43,081 --> 00:08:46,080
It also has accommodations to carry a large number of people.

107
00:08:46,081 --> 00:08:49,080
They have extra gas tanks built into the aircraft

108
00:08:49,081 --> 00:08:51,080
to extend the range of the aircraft.

109
00:08:51,081 --> 00:08:54,080
The most significant modification to the plane acknowledged

110
00:08:54,081 --> 00:08:58,080
another less publicized presidential mover,

111
00:08:58,081 --> 00:09:00,080
Roosevelt's wheelchair.

112
00:09:00,081 --> 00:09:03,080
They build an elevator into the back of the airplane.

113
00:09:03,081 --> 00:09:06,080
This way, President Roosevelt's wheelchair can be rolled onto the elevator,

114
00:09:06,081 --> 00:09:09,080
He could be raised into the aircraft.

115
00:09:09,081 --> 00:09:12,880
Roosevelt used his C-54, unofficially nicknamed

116
00:09:12,881 --> 00:09:17,320
the Sacred Cow, only once in February of 1945,

117
00:09:17,321 --> 00:09:21,320
traveling to an Allied conference at Yalta on the Black Sea.

118
00:09:21,321 --> 00:09:24,440
Harry S. Truman inherited the plane two months later,

119
00:09:24,441 --> 00:09:26,240
upon Roosevelt's death.

120
00:09:26,241 --> 00:09:29,040
He flew to Potsdam, Germany for the last of the wartime

121
00:09:29,041 --> 00:09:32,440
conferences in July of 1945.

122
00:09:32,441 --> 00:09:38,820
And Truman routinely conducted business on board the presidential aircraft.

123
00:09:38,821 --> 00:09:48,140
He signed the National Security Act of 1947, which created the Defense Department, the Air Force, and the CIA, among many other things.

124
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:53,820
And it was an example of conduct of presidential business while flying.

125
00:09:55,500 --> 00:10:00,180
Aviation technology had advanced rapidly by the time Truman was signing laws in midair,

126
00:10:00,181 --> 00:10:04,140
and the sacred cow had been replaced by a new DC-6

127
00:10:04,141 --> 00:10:05,720
with a pressurized cabin.

128
00:10:06,920 --> 00:10:09,780
This is an important step up in technology

129
00:10:09,781 --> 00:10:13,160
because this airplane was larger, had greater range,

130
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:17,540
and really represented a new level of flying comfort for the president.

131
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:25,260
And the livery of this plane, the Independence, was unique.

132
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:28,440
It has the stylized look of an eagle.

133
00:10:28,441 --> 00:10:34,360
The cockpit nose looks like the beak of the eagle and the feathers going down the fuselage and so forth.

134
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:36,680
It had a real exotic look to it.

135
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:40,620
Maybe a little too exotic.

136
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:44,900
Early color photos of the livery or external appearance of the plane

137
00:10:44,901 --> 00:10:48,620
reveal a yellow paint scheme that was soon changed.

138
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:55,280
President Truman's aircraft, the Independents, had in the nose a weather radar designed to help it navigate.

139
00:10:55,281 --> 00:10:58,220
And paint can and does interfere with radar,

140
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:04,160
and it is very possible that the color of the nose of Truman's airplane was changed

141
00:11:04,161 --> 00:11:07,280
so that the weather radar on the plane would work better.

142
00:11:08,440 --> 00:11:12,500
The independents flew Truman to Wake Island in October of 1950

143
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:15,040
to confront General Douglas MacArthur,

144
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:18,320
who refused to follow administration policy in Korea.

145
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:23,600
This presidential journey began one of the greatest crises of Truman's presidency,

146
00:11:23,601 --> 00:11:26,200
which culminated in his firing MacArthur.

147
00:11:28,660 --> 00:11:31,060
Upon his election in 1952,

148
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:35,100
Dwight Eisenhower became the first president who knew how to fly a plane.

149
00:11:35,800 --> 00:11:38,400
The Air Force kept ahead of the technology curve

150
00:11:38,401 --> 00:11:42,120
by transforming a new Lockheed 749 Constellation

151
00:11:42,121 --> 00:11:44,300
into the official presidential plane.

152
00:11:45,680 --> 00:11:48,560
Upgraded in 1954 to a super-constellation,

153
00:11:49,220 --> 00:11:51,340
these planes were the first presidential aircraft

154
00:11:51,341 --> 00:11:54,440
with a private presidential suite and a conference table.

155
00:11:55,500 --> 00:12:00,220
The Columbine 3, which is the one that's on display at the United States Air Force Museum,

156
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:04,600
this aircraft had the latest in technology available for the President of the United States.

157
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:08,800
He could make a telephone call from the airplane and be connected to someone on the ground.

158
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:10,600
Plus, he had a teletype machine.

159
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:17,520
Despite the improved technology on board Columbine 3,

160
00:12:17,521 --> 00:12:22,280
the piston-driven constellations were far behind the new jet-powered aircraft

161
00:12:22,281 --> 00:12:26,420
that were overtaking the commercial airline industry in the mid-1950s.

162
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:34,680
The office of the President of the United States needed the biggest, fastest, most advanced plane

163
00:12:34,681 --> 00:12:35,431
available.

164
00:12:35,432 --> 00:12:41,740
In 1959, President Eisenhower would become a presidential traveler on the first in a

165
00:12:41,741 --> 00:12:45,900
line of presidential jets.

166
00:12:45,901 --> 00:12:48,700
So confident was the Air Force that Harry Truman would lose

167
00:12:48,701 --> 00:12:52,860
the 1948 presidential election that it remodeled a Constellation

168
00:12:52,861 --> 00:12:57,300
four engine nicknamed the Dewdrop for his opponent, Thomas E.

169
00:12:57,301 --> 00:12:58,860
Dewey.

170
00:12:58,861 --> 00:13:01,780
Presidential movers will return on Modern Marvels.

171
00:13:07,020 --> 00:13:09,520
Today's Air Force One jumbo jets are the latest

172
00:13:09,521 --> 00:13:14,820
in a series of jet-powered aircraft that began in the late 1950s.

173
00:13:14,821 --> 00:13:16,920
The switch from piston-driven aircraft

174
00:13:16,921 --> 00:13:20,700
was a product of technology and Cold War one-upmanship.

175
00:13:20,701 --> 00:13:22,700
Premier Khrushchev had a jet.

176
00:13:22,701 --> 00:13:25,700
Therefore, the State Department convinced the President

177
00:13:25,701 --> 00:13:27,200
of the United States and Congress

178
00:13:27,201 --> 00:13:28,240
that the President of the United States

179
00:13:28,241 --> 00:13:30,180
needed to move up to jet aircraft.

180
00:13:30,181 --> 00:13:33,880
The aircraft was a Boeing 707,

181
00:13:33,881 --> 00:13:37,620
known to the military as a C-137A.

182
00:13:37,621 --> 00:13:45,220
It was not built and earmarked specifically for presidential travel, but Eisenhower flew on this aircraft.

183
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:54,780
Tail number Sam for Special Air Mission 709 was the first plane designated with a radio call sign Air Force One.

184
00:13:56,680 --> 00:14:03,340
Initially, an airplane was named based upon the mission number the airplane was flying.

185
00:14:03,341 --> 00:14:05,400
So the number would change periodically.

186
00:14:05,401 --> 00:14:13,560
Unfortunately, one day, President Eisenhower was using a number very similar to one being used by an Eastern Airlines aircraft,

187
00:14:13,561 --> 00:14:17,540
and there was some confusion as to which aircraft the president of the United States was on.

188
00:14:17,780 --> 00:14:23,280
To make certain that never happened again, the Air Force adopted a code word, a secret code word, Air Force One.

189
00:14:25,340 --> 00:14:30,140
The name carried over even to the smaller Air Force planes the president occasionally used.

190
00:14:30,141 --> 00:14:35,740
But the secret got out, and the term Air Force One caught on.

191
00:14:36,380 --> 00:14:37,640
So did 707s.

192
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:43,440
Everybody recognized this aircraft as leading all others in advancement aviation.

193
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:44,900
This was the aircraft.

194
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:54,100
A new 707, tail number Sam 26000, joined a new president,

195
00:14:54,380 --> 00:14:58,700
who recognized that the orange Air Force security paint job had to go.

196
00:14:58,701 --> 00:15:09,815
The Kennedys, with their interest in style and their flair for that, enlisted Raymond Lowy, who was an industrial designer, a very famous one, to see if he could come

197
00:15:09,816 --> 00:15:12,560
up with a new livery, a new paint scheme.

198
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:24,468
The light blue, silver, and white livery proudly displaying United States of America with the American flag on the tail and the presidential seal on the door was

199
00:15:24,469 --> 00:15:25,169
iconic.

200
00:15:25,170 --> 00:15:30,140
I think it's a paint scheme that just really fits the airplane.

201
00:15:31,100 --> 00:15:34,940
I personally think it fit the 707 to a T.

202
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:41,300
The new Air Force One had style and substance over the Eisenhower-era Columbine III,

203
00:15:41,700 --> 00:15:43,480
which Kennedy still had access to.

204
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:49,220
The difference between these two aircraft is like night and day.

205
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:55,480
The 707 is much faster, going along at about 550 miles an hour as opposed to 300 miles an hour.

206
00:15:55,820 --> 00:15:57,600
It is truly transcontinental.

207
00:15:58,160 --> 00:16:04,820
The aircraft cannot be refueled in flight, but it carries enough fuel that it can get across the Atlantic and well into any place it needs to go.

208
00:16:06,100 --> 00:16:10,800
SAM-26000 had the most up-to-date electronics and communication systems available.

209
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:14,440
It also flew higher and more smoothly than previous planes.

210
00:16:14,441 --> 00:16:20,560
Kennedy's original presidential suite gave the chief executive some privacy,

211
00:16:20,561 --> 00:16:23,880
with a bedroom in addition to plush, comfortable chairs.

212
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:28,940
In 1963, the plane entered the record books.

213
00:16:29,900 --> 00:16:34,540
President Kennedy wasn't on board, but a U.S. delegation flew to Moscow in 1963,

214
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:40,740
and they broke 30 world records, including the fastest time from the United States to the Soviet Union.

215
00:16:40,741 --> 00:16:44,980
In June of that year, Kennedy took Sam 2-6000

216
00:16:44,981 --> 00:16:46,460
for his dramatic visit to Berlin.

217
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:50,780
But the jet made a more memorable impression in Dallas

218
00:16:50,781 --> 00:16:52,220
on November 22nd.

219
00:16:52,540 --> 00:16:54,580
And it will be only a matter of minutes

220
00:16:54,581 --> 00:16:56,220
before he arrives at the trade market.

221
00:16:56,600 --> 00:16:58,360
Something has happened in the motor stage.

222
00:16:58,500 --> 00:16:59,360
Stand by, please.

223
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:03,180
Members of the crew listened in shock

224
00:17:03,181 --> 00:17:05,780
as news of the president's assassination was broadcast.

225
00:17:06,920 --> 00:17:09,900
It had been announced that they thought

226
00:17:09,901 --> 00:17:12,900
that the president was not going to make it,

227
00:17:13,460 --> 00:17:16,040
and it was unbelievable.

228
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:21,320
Within hours, Air Force One was transformed into a beer

229
00:17:21,321 --> 00:17:24,520
to carry the president's body back to Washington, D.C.

230
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:29,820
It just hit me that we cannot, you know,

231
00:17:29,900 --> 00:17:32,740
put the president's body in the cargo compartment.

232
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,540
And then I made the suggestion that,

233
00:17:35,541 --> 00:17:38,660
I said, well, we've got to put the casket in the cabin,

234
00:17:38,661 --> 00:17:40,460
and there was some discussion

235
00:17:40,461 --> 00:17:42,220
and a little disagreement.

236
00:17:42,500 --> 00:17:43,440
It won't fit.

237
00:17:43,580 --> 00:17:44,720
It won't go over the casket.

238
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:46,400
And I said, yes, it will.

239
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:51,160
After the casket was placed inside the plane,

240
00:17:51,880 --> 00:17:53,900
Lyndon Johnson insisted he be sworn in

241
00:17:53,901 --> 00:17:55,260
before the plane took off,

242
00:17:55,680 --> 00:17:57,200
with Mrs. Kennedy by his side.

243
00:17:58,060 --> 00:18:00,100
And that's one of the most searing

244
00:18:00,101 --> 00:18:02,460
and memorable photographs in American history

245
00:18:02,461 --> 00:18:04,780
of Jacqueline Kennedy standing there

246
00:18:04,781 --> 00:18:08,220
with that blood-splattered outfit on.

247
00:18:08,221 --> 00:18:14,480
So there's Air Force One, and it's at the epicenter of the American experience at that point.

248
00:18:15,540 --> 00:18:19,680
As president, Johnson altered SAM-26000 to fit his style,

249
00:18:20,380 --> 00:18:24,560
removing partitions to better see what was going on from his suite at the rear of the plane.

250
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:30,460
Johnson also used the plane as leverage, offering an exclusive ride as a political plum.

251
00:18:31,540 --> 00:18:33,140
Twice he visited troops in Vietnam.

252
00:18:33,141 --> 00:18:37,140
When Richard Nixon took office in 1969,

253
00:18:37,141 --> 00:18:40,140
Sam 26000 was being renovated.

254
00:18:40,141 --> 00:18:43,140
They literally stripped the airplane on the inside

255
00:18:43,141 --> 00:18:46,140
from the very nose to the very tail of the aircraft.

256
00:18:46,141 --> 00:18:49,140
They took out everything, put new wiring, new electronics,

257
00:18:49,141 --> 00:18:54,140
and by doing that, it made it a true working White House.

258
00:18:54,141 --> 00:19:00,140
A new Boeing 707, Sam 27000, joined the fleet in 1972.

259
00:19:00,141 --> 00:19:06,620
1972, and together, the planes helped Nixon conduct foreign policy in Russia and China.

260
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:14,120
But in 1974, Nixon resigned the presidency amidst the Watergate scandal.

261
00:19:15,040 --> 00:19:18,500
I shall resign the presidency effective at noon tomorrow.

262
00:19:20,180 --> 00:19:26,880
Vice President Ford will be sworn in as president at that hour in this office.

263
00:19:26,881 --> 00:19:34,100
He left Washington for the last time as president on August 9th, traveling back home to California.

264
00:19:35,300 --> 00:19:40,960
The pilot took note of the fact that Gerald Ford would be sworn in as president around noon,

265
00:19:41,220 --> 00:19:46,280
and the plane was over Kansas City traffic control at that very moment,

266
00:19:46,281 --> 00:19:53,300
and the pilot called down, and the designator ceased to be Air Force One and became Sam 26,000.

267
00:19:53,800 --> 00:19:56,080
President Nixon then became Citizen Nixon.

268
00:19:56,880 --> 00:20:01,760
My instructions were that at noon when everything was to pass,

269
00:20:01,940 --> 00:20:04,720
they were going to have a martini together.

270
00:20:04,940 --> 00:20:09,660
They did have a martini at the passing of the guard, so to speak.

271
00:20:10,600 --> 00:20:14,400
The symbolic power of Air Force One backfired for President Ford

272
00:20:14,401 --> 00:20:17,980
when a stumble down the steps in front of the plane with Mrs. Ford

273
00:20:17,981 --> 00:20:20,520
became an emblematic image of his presidency.

274
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:25,540
One of the stewards would try to hold the umbrella for her

275
00:20:25,541 --> 00:20:28,240
and another steward for the president.

276
00:20:28,360 --> 00:20:31,080
But President Ford refused that day.

277
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:32,160
He said, I'll take the umbrella.

278
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:36,240
And he slipped a little bit and thought I'd lost my job.

279
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:37,920
Got a few phone calls that evening.

280
00:20:39,180 --> 00:20:41,760
Both presidents Carter and Reagan

281
00:20:41,761 --> 00:20:44,580
used Air Force One as a diplomatic tool

282
00:20:44,581 --> 00:20:47,400
and to symbolize the strength and power of the presidency,

283
00:20:48,200 --> 00:20:51,300
most memorably during Reagan's visit to Berlin in 1987,

284
00:20:52,020 --> 00:20:54,440
an echo of Kennedy's trip 25 years earlier.

285
00:20:54,441 --> 00:21:01,160
Sam 27,000 took Ronald Reagan to Berlin, too, to make another epic speech.

286
00:21:01,161 --> 00:21:05,040
Mr. Gorbachev, open this gate.

287
00:21:05,041 --> 00:21:09,160
And that's another way of looking at Air Force One.

288
00:21:09,161 --> 00:21:14,880
It's kind of an arrow going through the sky, taking presidents to very pivotal meetings.

289
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:19,640
By the end of Reagan's term, Air Force One needed an upgrade,

290
00:21:20,060 --> 00:21:22,720
and he signed the order for the current 747s.

291
00:21:22,721 --> 00:21:24,720
Good heavens.

292
00:21:24,721 --> 00:21:26,720
Rookie setup.

293
00:21:26,721 --> 00:21:28,720
I just want a peek.

294
00:21:28,721 --> 00:21:30,720
Isn't this beautiful?

295
00:21:30,721 --> 00:21:32,720
See the hot tub?

296
00:21:32,721 --> 00:21:34,720
No hot tub.

297
00:21:34,721 --> 00:21:40,414
Delivered after the Reagan term, they have served Presidents Bush Sr., Clinton, and George W.

298
00:21:40,415 --> 00:21:41,115
Bush.

299
00:21:41,116 --> 00:21:44,720
It seems like we've reached technologically kind of a plateau.

300
00:21:44,721 --> 00:21:51,620
So the current 747s represent, in terms of speed and accommodations

301
00:21:51,621 --> 00:21:56,840
and technical sophistication, everything you would need.

302
00:21:59,600 --> 00:22:01,140
More than any of the other vehicles

303
00:22:01,141 --> 00:22:03,920
that transport the President of the United States,

304
00:22:03,921 --> 00:22:05,880
Air Force One has become an extension

305
00:22:05,881 --> 00:22:09,380
of the President's power and influence.

306
00:22:09,381 --> 00:22:13,920
You've got this beacon of freedom landing on foreign soil

307
00:22:13,921 --> 00:22:17,360
taking one of the most powerful men in the world

308
00:22:17,361 --> 00:22:19,200
to their particular country.

309
00:22:19,201 --> 00:22:23,400
So I think the symbolism is hugely important.

310
00:22:23,401 --> 00:22:27,460
When that airplane pulls up to the spot for arrival

311
00:22:27,461 --> 00:22:31,200
and the door comes open and the president comes out,

312
00:22:31,201 --> 00:22:35,680
I think that really symbolizes the strength of our country.

313
00:22:35,681 --> 00:22:38,240
Obviously, we have many symbols of strength in the country,

314
00:22:38,241 --> 00:22:43,520
but I don't think any show more strength than Air Force One.

315
00:22:43,521 --> 00:22:48,420
Long before Air Force One was sending presidents all over the world in the comfort of a flying office,

316
00:22:49,080 --> 00:22:52,460
chief executives got by with less efficient modes of transportation.

317
00:22:53,460 --> 00:22:58,600
For the first presidents, the horse and carriage represented the pinnacle of speed and comfort.

318
00:23:01,480 --> 00:23:05,920
The total cost of both of the Air Force One jumbo jet 747s,

319
00:23:05,921 --> 00:23:12,300
including all the top-secret equipment, plus a specially designed hangar, is over $500 million.

320
00:23:12,301 --> 00:23:17,820
dollars presidential movers will return on modern marvels

321
00:23:22,540 --> 00:23:27,580
the revolution in transportation over the last century has allowed the president a variety of

322
00:23:27,581 --> 00:23:33,100
options planes trains and automobiles that simply didn't exist in 1789

323
00:23:35,260 --> 00:23:39,740
for george washington transportation wasn't all that different from that of the roman emperors

324
00:23:39,741 --> 00:23:42,020
and it invariably involved horses.

325
00:23:42,820 --> 00:23:45,680
He went up and journeyed through New England with Thomas Jefferson.

326
00:23:46,420 --> 00:23:48,440
And he did this in his diaries.

327
00:23:48,560 --> 00:23:53,540
He talks about how he was there not for his own personal benefit or enjoyment.

328
00:23:53,920 --> 00:23:56,700
He just wanted the country to share the experience of him as president.

329
00:23:58,160 --> 00:24:00,780
Most early presidents, however, kept near the capital.

330
00:24:01,800 --> 00:24:03,100
James Monroe was an exception.

331
00:24:03,960 --> 00:24:08,180
In 1817 and 1819, he made two arduous carriage journeys

332
00:24:08,181 --> 00:24:10,760
through the north and south to inspect defenses.

333
00:24:12,240 --> 00:24:13,680
Monroe paid for both trips

334
00:24:13,681 --> 00:24:16,640
because there was no official budget for such excursions.

335
00:24:17,340 --> 00:24:20,620
In Boston, it's reported that 40,000 people

336
00:24:20,621 --> 00:24:23,320
traveled into the city just to get a peek at their president.

337
00:24:24,360 --> 00:24:28,120
When Lincoln, a promoter of railroads, took office in 1861,

338
00:24:28,900 --> 00:24:30,620
rails were tying the continent together.

339
00:24:31,120 --> 00:24:32,760
He used it to his benefit.

340
00:24:32,900 --> 00:24:34,500
He used it to get up to Gettysburg, for example.

341
00:24:34,860 --> 00:24:36,080
Wrote a little speech on the way there.

342
00:24:36,660 --> 00:24:38,260
For Lincoln, there's a lot of safety concerns.

343
00:24:38,600 --> 00:24:41,280
Very famously, he snuck into Washington as president-elect

344
00:24:41,281 --> 00:24:44,500
because it was perceived there was a plot in Baltimore to assassinate him.

345
00:24:45,320 --> 00:24:51,220
In 1864, Congress ordered a special rail car to safely transport Lincoln during the Civil War,

346
00:24:51,660 --> 00:24:55,400
the first vehicle built for a president and paid for by the government.

347
00:24:56,160 --> 00:25:00,100
It's a magnificent car, and he doesn't get to ride in it until he's shot and he's dead.

348
00:25:00,200 --> 00:25:01,000
He can no longer enjoy it.

349
00:25:01,001 --> 00:25:04,120
But that car made it one of the most famous train journeys of a president ever,

350
00:25:04,121 --> 00:25:08,620
slowly with the bell chiming and constantly all the way back to Springfield, Illinois,

351
00:25:08,780 --> 00:25:12,280
carrying Lincoln's body and the country mourning alongside the road.

352
00:25:14,780 --> 00:25:18,780
Rutherford B. Hayes achieved an important presidential milestone in 1880

353
00:25:18,781 --> 00:25:20,520
during one of his many travels.

354
00:25:21,420 --> 00:25:23,620
They called him Rutherford the Rover,

355
00:25:23,940 --> 00:25:28,800
and one of his achievements was that he took a train and went to the West Coast.

356
00:25:29,820 --> 00:25:32,080
And that act is incredibly symbolic.

357
00:25:32,081 --> 00:25:36,080
Now the president has gone to the people in Oregon and California.

358
00:25:36,081 --> 00:25:39,080
Not only can they go to him, but now he has gone to them.

359
00:25:42,080 --> 00:25:47,080
After Hays, luxury loaners from railroad magnates whisked presidents across America.

360
00:25:49,080 --> 00:25:57,080
Not until World War II did Congress agree to pay for a specially reinforced custom Pullman rail car for the president's safety.

361
00:25:57,081 --> 00:26:02,760
They put in three-inch thick glass.

362
00:26:02,761 --> 00:26:07,020
They also added 5-8-inch armor plating to the car and all the

363
00:26:07,021 --> 00:26:09,020
areas where the president would normally be.

364
00:26:09,021 --> 00:26:11,720
They also added two escape hatches to the car.

365
00:26:11,721 --> 00:26:16,760
One is in the roof of the lounge, and the other is in the outer

366
00:26:16,761 --> 00:26:19,940
wall of the bathroom.

367
00:26:19,941 --> 00:26:24,080
Named Ferdinand Magellan, the 84-foot-long,

368
00:26:24,081 --> 00:26:29,320
long 145-ton car had telephones in every room, an observation

369
00:26:29,321 --> 00:26:32,960
lounge, dining room, and four bedrooms.

370
00:26:32,961 --> 00:26:35,920
President Franklin Roosevelt had a special narrow wheelchair

371
00:26:35,921 --> 00:26:37,460
designed for use within its hallways.

372
00:26:40,220 --> 00:26:43,440
After FDR's death, the car was used in the funeral train

373
00:26:43,441 --> 00:26:45,800
that brought his body back to Washington,

374
00:26:45,801 --> 00:26:49,460
echoing Lincoln's journey 80 years earlier.

375
00:26:49,461 --> 00:26:51,840
Mrs. Roosevelt sat in the Magellan,

376
00:26:51,841 --> 00:26:54,120
while Roosevelt's body was carried in another car.

377
00:26:56,040 --> 00:26:59,200
President Truman, who succeeded President Roosevelt,

378
00:26:59,700 --> 00:27:02,060
did use the car, again, quite extensively,

379
00:27:02,360 --> 00:27:04,500
especially in the election campaign,

380
00:27:04,680 --> 00:27:07,240
where he did over 300 speeches from the rear of the Magellan.

381
00:27:08,840 --> 00:27:11,200
Truman's whistle-stop campaign from the Magellan

382
00:27:11,201 --> 00:27:14,620
was one factor in his upset win against Dewey in 1948.

383
00:27:16,240 --> 00:27:20,200
It was also the setting for a famous photograph of a presidential mover

384
00:27:20,201 --> 00:27:22,300
and a journalistic blunder.

385
00:27:23,540 --> 00:27:27,420
That famous photograph was taken of President Truman

386
00:27:27,421 --> 00:27:30,580
at St. Louis Union Station after the elections,

387
00:27:31,380 --> 00:27:36,100
and Mr. Truman had a great time with that newspaper in St. Louis that day.

388
00:27:37,980 --> 00:27:41,940
The Magellan has been retired as a presidential mover for half a century,

389
00:27:42,840 --> 00:27:44,780
done in by the rise of the automobile.

390
00:27:46,460 --> 00:27:49,840
Now on exhibit at the Gold Coast Railroad Museum in Miami,

391
00:27:50,200 --> 00:27:53,600
it represents the last of the more leisurely presidential movers.

392
00:27:54,840 --> 00:27:59,180
Another leisurely presidential mover is really more of a presidential retreat.

393
00:27:59,760 --> 00:28:03,939
The presidential yachts, which began with a steamer used by Rutherford B.

394
00:28:03,940 --> 00:28:04,740
Hayes in 1880,

395
00:28:05,180 --> 00:28:08,520
aren't so much modes of transportation as modes of recreation.

396
00:28:09,340 --> 00:28:14,100
The yachts have kind of a party-like ambiance to them.

397
00:28:14,101 --> 00:28:18,560
You know, it's a place where you go for meetings or kind of a leisurely cruise.

398
00:28:18,561 --> 00:28:24,000
but I don't think they fall into the category of practical, essential travel.

399
00:28:26,240 --> 00:28:30,240
Presidents have officially entertained on them, as Truman did on the Williamsburg,

400
00:28:30,560 --> 00:28:34,980
and presidents have escaped from their burdens on them, as Kennedy did on the Honey Fitz.

401
00:28:36,460 --> 00:28:38,560
The LBJ loved to play poker on this yacht.

402
00:28:38,840 --> 00:28:41,340
He'd bring his congressman on one of the yachts and tell him,

403
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:44,420
gentlemen, we're not leaving until you agree with me.

404
00:28:44,421 --> 00:28:50,800
But there was always the nagging debate that they were an expensive luxury of an imperial presidency.

405
00:28:51,940 --> 00:28:57,860
Carter ended the debate in the late 1970s when he ordered the USS Sequoia auctioned off.

406
00:28:59,460 --> 00:29:05,500
If yachts best facilitated the president's escape from the people, then automobiles did just the opposite.

407
00:29:06,380 --> 00:29:11,400
No mode of transportation better allows the president to impart a message to his people than the car.

408
00:29:11,401 --> 00:29:16,400
It's personal, it's intimate, and it's also a security nightmare.

409
00:29:16,401 --> 00:29:20,400
A battle between the president's need to be seen and to be safe

410
00:29:20,401 --> 00:29:23,400
is waged around the automobile.

411
00:29:27,400 --> 00:29:31,400
President Ulysses S. Grant received a speeding ticket from a traffic cop

412
00:29:31,401 --> 00:29:34,400
for going too fast in his horse-drawn carriage.

413
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:39,400
Presidential Movers will return on Modern Marvels.

414
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:47,860
George W. Bush rode to his January 2001 inaugural in an elegant black Cadillac

415
00:29:47,861 --> 00:29:53,800
DeVille stretch limousine. Or at least that's what it looked like. This thing is

416
00:29:53,801 --> 00:29:57,520
its own beast. Cadillac is the builder of the current limousines for the president.

417
00:29:57,521 --> 00:30:01,720
It has to make the car look like a Cadillac but of course it's not. I think

418
00:30:01,721 --> 00:30:07,920
down to the ornament on the hood is oversized. Details are classified but you

419
00:30:07,921 --> 00:30:14,120
You can bet the car is armored, contains bulletproof glass, and has run-flat tires.

420
00:30:14,121 --> 00:30:20,160
Surely it hides defenses within its chassis, uses an air filtration system, and electromagnetic

421
00:30:20,161 --> 00:30:22,360
pulse defenses.

422
00:30:22,361 --> 00:30:27,480
It likely has night vision technology that projects infrared images.

423
00:30:27,481 --> 00:30:29,720
And that's just the car.

424
00:30:29,721 --> 00:30:35,460
There's also a driver, a very good driver, trained to flee any danger.

425
00:30:35,461 --> 00:30:40,060
The fleet of cars at the Bush administration's disposal is part of a century-old tradition

426
00:30:40,061 --> 00:30:45,160
of presidential transport that began with William McKinley, who became the first president

427
00:30:45,161 --> 00:30:52,100
to travel in an automobile, a Stanley Steamer in 1899.

428
00:30:52,101 --> 00:30:56,580
But it was William Howard Taft who established the car as a presidential mover.

429
00:30:56,581 --> 00:31:02,560
He convinces Congress to authorize within the normal $25,000 travel appropriation, which

430
00:31:02,561 --> 00:31:06,960
which authorizes expense upon horses and carriages that Roosevelt had,

431
00:31:07,160 --> 00:31:11,080
he gets Congress to change that to include and automobiles.

432
00:31:12,020 --> 00:31:14,000
Now, Congress went crazy.

433
00:31:14,380 --> 00:31:16,980
Well, the winning argument, what was said to be the winning argument,

434
00:31:17,100 --> 00:31:21,220
was that it would be cruelty to animals to have Taft be carried around by a horse.

435
00:31:21,420 --> 00:31:24,620
But really, the winning argument was that the president wanted these automobiles,

436
00:31:24,900 --> 00:31:26,700
and the president was going to get them.

437
00:31:27,360 --> 00:31:29,360
Due in part to Taft's endorsement,

438
00:31:29,361 --> 00:31:32,780
the auto industry doubled production during his first year in office.

439
00:31:33,460 --> 00:31:36,140
He literally put the presidential seal on the auto,

440
00:31:36,700 --> 00:31:38,260
displaying it on his favorite car,

441
00:31:38,860 --> 00:31:44,380
a 1909 White Company Model M 7-passenger, 40-horsepower steamer,

442
00:31:44,620 --> 00:31:48,200
one of the most technologically advanced automobiles of the day.

443
00:31:48,780 --> 00:31:51,840
The White Company bragged that the car that the president has

444
00:31:51,841 --> 00:31:54,040
is the same as any car he can get from us.

445
00:31:54,120 --> 00:31:56,360
So the president's taking a car right off the shelf, basically.

446
00:31:56,361 --> 00:32:01,360
Under Taft, the White House stables were converted into a garage.

447
00:32:01,361 --> 00:32:07,360
In 1921, Warren G. Harding became the first president to motor to his inauguration.

448
00:32:07,361 --> 00:32:17,360
But it was FDR who in the 1930s received the first armored vehicle, a limousine.

449
00:32:17,361 --> 00:32:22,360
Among the fleet of limousines specially built for chief executives since 1942,

450
00:32:22,361 --> 00:32:23,860
Four stand out.

451
00:32:24,320 --> 00:32:26,300
The first is FDR's Lincoln K.

452
00:32:26,940 --> 00:32:29,440
Initially, it had no armor or bulletproof glass.

453
00:32:30,560 --> 00:32:37,380
It was after Pearl Harbor that the car was armor-plated,

454
00:32:38,080 --> 00:32:42,880
although it never had a permanent roof.

455
00:32:42,881 --> 00:32:47,740
And Franklin Roosevelt enjoyed very much riding with the top down.

456
00:32:48,020 --> 00:32:51,560
That's apparently where the nickname the Sunshine Special came from.

457
00:32:51,561 --> 00:32:54,780
This car became an emblem of his administration.

458
00:32:55,060 --> 00:32:56,480
It was the way people saw him.

459
00:32:56,540 --> 00:32:58,000
He actually praised his chauffeur.

460
00:32:58,140 --> 00:33:02,160
He said, Monty can drive through a crowd of 30,000s and not step on one toe.

461
00:33:02,400 --> 00:33:08,400
And so his chauffeur was an expert at driving through people crowding around the car trying to reach the president.

462
00:33:11,240 --> 00:33:17,000
Eisenhower introduced the bubble top, a clear plastic shell that fit over his convertible parade car,

463
00:33:17,360 --> 00:33:20,680
so the people who braved bad weather to see him wouldn't be disappointed.

464
00:33:21,560 --> 00:33:24,440
It was actually a car that was built for Harry Truman.

465
00:33:24,940 --> 00:33:29,660
It was a 1949 Lincoln, another open car.

466
00:33:30,140 --> 00:33:31,560
That car was used by Truman.

467
00:33:31,680 --> 00:33:32,740
It was used by Eisenhower.

468
00:33:32,820 --> 00:33:34,540
It was actually used into the Kennedy administration.

469
00:33:35,500 --> 00:33:37,980
That car was completely un-armor-plated,

470
00:33:38,340 --> 00:33:41,480
no bulletproof glass, no armor, no nothing.

471
00:33:42,620 --> 00:33:44,620
At that time in American history,

472
00:33:45,160 --> 00:33:46,920
the president's political necessities

473
00:33:46,921 --> 00:33:48,700
trumped his security requirements.

474
00:33:49,620 --> 00:33:53,380
Well-trained drivers still took the wheel and Secret Service men ran alongside.

475
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:57,180
But the president straddled the breach of his two competing needs.

476
00:33:57,940 --> 00:34:02,800
Now, if we left it up to the Secret Service to decide what the president's car looks like, it won't have any windows.

477
00:34:03,540 --> 00:34:06,360
Windows are a safety concession to politics.

478
00:34:06,540 --> 00:34:10,120
The Secret Service, of course, wants to keep the president hermetically sealed.

479
00:34:10,300 --> 00:34:11,460
There's a constant tension.

480
00:34:11,461 --> 00:34:17,460
The most famous presidential limousine of all

481
00:34:17,461 --> 00:34:19,260
graphically illustrated this point.

482
00:34:20,480 --> 00:34:23,980
The car, a four-ton Lincoln Continental Stretch X100,

483
00:34:24,700 --> 00:34:29,620
cost the government nearly $195,000 to retrofit for President Kennedy.

484
00:34:30,340 --> 00:34:33,460
It was one of 131 vehicles in his fleet.

485
00:34:35,440 --> 00:34:38,960
Some of its safety features were two rear-facing jump seats,

486
00:34:39,560 --> 00:34:41,560
outside handles for Secret Service agents,

487
00:34:42,260 --> 00:34:43,480
retractable running boards,

488
00:34:44,340 --> 00:34:46,720
a presidential seat that could be raised and lowered,

489
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:48,820
and a tight turning radius.

490
00:34:49,280 --> 00:34:51,980
And as we see in 1963, if you take the roof off,

491
00:34:52,120 --> 00:34:53,080
it all comes to nothing.

492
00:34:54,920 --> 00:34:56,840
John F. Kennedy was assassinated

493
00:34:56,841 --> 00:34:59,360
as he sat in his unarmored Stretch X-100,

494
00:35:00,080 --> 00:35:02,140
caught between politics and safety.

495
00:35:03,320 --> 00:35:06,480
Rarely has a single event had such far-reaching impact.

496
00:35:06,481 --> 00:35:10,720
The Secret Service was in a quandary.

497
00:35:10,860 --> 00:35:14,280
They now demanded that the president have an armored car.

498
00:35:15,180 --> 00:35:16,860
And they had a choice.

499
00:35:17,220 --> 00:35:19,900
They could either build a brand new car

500
00:35:19,901 --> 00:35:23,260
or they could modify the existing car.

501
00:35:24,040 --> 00:35:27,360
The reason the Secret Service rebuilt the Stretch X100

502
00:35:27,361 --> 00:35:30,640
rather than order and customize a new one was cost.

503
00:35:31,700 --> 00:35:34,540
After the Warren Commission examined and documented it,

504
00:35:34,541 --> 00:35:36,620
The car was released to the Secret Service.

505
00:35:37,340 --> 00:35:39,320
It still cost a million dollars to renovate,

506
00:35:39,960 --> 00:35:45,780
including a $125,000, 1,500-pound windshield of bulletproof glass.

507
00:35:47,260 --> 00:35:49,920
They stripped the car down mechanically.

508
00:35:49,921 --> 00:35:53,960
They welded on a permanent armor-plated roof.

509
00:35:54,260 --> 00:35:56,180
They armor-plated the body.

510
00:35:56,340 --> 00:36:01,360
They armor-plated the doors all around the passenger compartment underneath the car.

511
00:36:01,361 --> 00:36:05,060
they had an early version of run-flat tires.

512
00:36:05,480 --> 00:36:08,840
It has communications systems and radios and such inside

513
00:36:08,841 --> 00:36:10,780
so that you can communicate with the Secret Service,

514
00:36:10,960 --> 00:36:12,060
the helicopters up above.

515
00:36:12,200 --> 00:36:14,700
So it was a thoroughgoing rebuild of the car.

516
00:36:15,380 --> 00:36:20,180
It continued to serve Presidents Johnson, Nixon, Ford, and Carter.

517
00:36:21,300 --> 00:36:23,600
President Nixon even cut a hole in the roof

518
00:36:23,601 --> 00:36:25,680
so he could stand up and wave to crowds,

519
00:36:26,480 --> 00:36:28,660
thoroughly compromising all of the reinforcements

520
00:36:28,661 --> 00:36:30,100
added after the Kennedy assassination.

521
00:36:31,360 --> 00:36:36,800
The last of the four famous historical presidential limousines was built for Gerald Ford in 1972.

522
00:36:38,520 --> 00:36:43,320
Weighing in at 13,000 pounds, it could resist a grenade thrown beneath it.

523
00:36:43,940 --> 00:36:50,620
It's come to be called a Reagan car because it was the car that Ronald Reagan was getting into when he was shot.

524
00:36:51,260 --> 00:36:55,540
And it shows the limits of the protection afforded by a car.

525
00:36:55,780 --> 00:36:57,740
This car is thoroughly armor-plagued.

526
00:36:57,741 --> 00:37:02,080
Yet Reagan wasn't inside the car.

527
00:37:02,240 --> 00:37:04,840
The bullet bounced off the limousine.

528
00:37:04,960 --> 00:37:06,740
So good as the armor just bounced right off

529
00:37:06,741 --> 00:37:09,400
and landed in the president's chest, nearly killing him.

530
00:37:11,940 --> 00:37:14,640
The Reagan car joins the Sunshine Special,

531
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:16,880
the Bubble Top, and the Kennedy Limo

532
00:37:16,881 --> 00:37:19,600
on display at the Ford Museum in Dearborn, Michigan.

533
00:37:21,920 --> 00:37:24,880
But the president isn't limited to autos for short trips.

534
00:37:25,580 --> 00:37:27,340
There's also Marine One,

535
00:37:27,740 --> 00:37:31,180
the most technologically sophisticated helicopter in the world.

536
00:37:35,680 --> 00:37:39,100
Despite its $200,000 cost in 1961,

537
00:37:39,660 --> 00:37:42,840
the Ford Motor Company leased the Kennedy limousine to the White House

538
00:37:42,841 --> 00:37:45,060
for only $500 per year.

539
00:37:46,060 --> 00:37:48,780
Presidential movers will return on Modern Marvels.

540
00:37:52,760 --> 00:37:55,040
The current Marine One helicopter

541
00:37:55,041 --> 00:37:57,800
is the most agile of all the presidential movers,

542
00:37:58,440 --> 00:38:00,600
able to lift off from an airport tarmac

543
00:38:00,601 --> 00:38:03,520
and place the president back home on the lawn of the White House.

544
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:08,280
President Eisenhower ushered in presidential helicopter travel

545
00:38:08,281 --> 00:38:10,580
on July 12, 1957,

546
00:38:11,400 --> 00:38:14,680
in an attempt to simplify short trips to places like Camp David

547
00:38:14,681 --> 00:38:17,540
without involving the fleet of presidential limousines.

548
00:38:17,541 --> 00:38:21,800
The first presidential helicopter was a Bell H-13.

549
00:38:21,801 --> 00:38:27,880
that's the military version of the bubble-topped helicopter

550
00:38:27,881 --> 00:38:30,000
you see on the intro to MASH.

551
00:38:30,440 --> 00:38:32,860
There was very little space on the inside,

552
00:38:33,180 --> 00:38:37,320
and the Secret Service felt that it really wasn't safe for the president.

553
00:38:39,460 --> 00:38:42,560
Eisenhower's two-man H-13 was quickly replaced

554
00:38:42,561 --> 00:38:45,880
by a more spacious Sikorsky H-34C,

555
00:38:46,240 --> 00:38:48,460
which could accommodate half a dozen passengers,

556
00:38:48,840 --> 00:38:51,500
perfect for traveling the 70 miles to Camp David.

557
00:38:51,800 --> 00:38:56,460
It didn't require motorcades or stop traffic, and it was twice as fast as a limousine.

558
00:38:56,760 --> 00:39:01,380
Eisenhower saw the practicality of helicopters for short shuttle flights,

559
00:39:01,880 --> 00:39:04,680
and he liked the way you could get in and out of a place.

560
00:39:05,140 --> 00:39:07,900
And you think of the security measures, if you go by helicopter,

561
00:39:08,400 --> 00:39:13,080
that it arguably is more secure than moving in limousine across the landscape.

562
00:39:14,140 --> 00:39:16,500
But some presidential guests were leery.

563
00:39:17,160 --> 00:39:20,720
Soviet Premier Khrushchev refused Eisenhower's invitation for a ride

564
00:39:20,721 --> 00:39:22,980
during his United States visit in 1959.

565
00:39:23,520 --> 00:39:26,480
He really thought that this might be an effort

566
00:39:26,481 --> 00:39:29,360
to get him up in a helicopter and just crash the helicopter

567
00:39:29,361 --> 00:39:32,740
and get one major problem for the United States out of the way.

568
00:39:33,280 --> 00:39:34,800
But then to his surprise,

569
00:39:35,100 --> 00:39:37,600
President Eisenhower indicated he was going to go too.

570
00:39:37,980 --> 00:39:40,140
And so then Khrushchev immediately backed,

571
00:39:40,240 --> 00:39:41,900
oh, well, fine, I'm ready to go.

572
00:39:42,880 --> 00:39:45,220
Under Kennedy, the presidential choppers

573
00:39:45,221 --> 00:39:49,560
were the Sikorsky VH-34As, also known as H-3s.

574
00:39:49,561 --> 00:39:53,560
The H3 airframes were in service until 1975.

575
00:39:53,561 --> 00:39:57,560
It's probably one of the best known helicopters today.

576
00:39:57,561 --> 00:40:00,560
It was also one that's been around the longest.

577
00:40:00,561 --> 00:40:09,560
The 3A had an amphibious hull, so it had a capability of landing and taking off from the water for short periods of time.

578
00:40:09,561 --> 00:40:15,560
The rotor path on the 72-foot long craft had a 62-foot diameter.

579
00:40:15,561 --> 00:40:18,720
It sported two 1,400-horsepower engines.

580
00:40:19,460 --> 00:40:23,320
The cockpit had a large window with two large reclining chairs,

581
00:40:23,940 --> 00:40:27,640
and the navigational system and radios were the best available at the time.

582
00:40:28,200 --> 00:40:32,520
In the presidential cabin, they had the bench area

583
00:40:32,521 --> 00:40:35,220
where about five people in those days could sit.

584
00:40:36,320 --> 00:40:39,440
And then behind the president's in the next compartment,

585
00:40:39,700 --> 00:40:41,380
we had a small latrine.

586
00:40:41,381 --> 00:40:50,940
they also had a small galley aboard the plane marine one usually cruised at 135 miles per hour

587
00:40:50,941 --> 00:40:55,900
roughly a thousand feet in the air for flights that lasted between 30 minutes and an hour

588
00:40:55,901 --> 00:41:03,220
Kennedy even enjoyed showing his family how the choppers worked as the co-pilot was checking over

589
00:41:03,221 --> 00:41:10,000
around the helicopter he looked up and he saw the tail rotors wing and flapping back and forth and

590
00:41:10,001 --> 00:41:11,800
And he says, uh-oh, something's wrong with that.

591
00:41:11,860 --> 00:41:15,640
So he ran and looked in the cockpit, and there was John John,

592
00:41:15,940 --> 00:41:18,560
and John John was on the stick, and the president says,

593
00:41:18,620 --> 00:41:20,480
yeah, push it forward, then pull it back.

594
00:41:21,060 --> 00:41:23,580
So then the pilot says, Mr. President, he says,

595
00:41:23,820 --> 00:41:25,160
anything I can help you with?

596
00:41:25,180 --> 00:41:26,560
He says, no, no, we're getting along fine.

597
00:41:28,580 --> 00:41:30,620
Although the Marines are closely associated

598
00:41:30,621 --> 00:41:32,240
with the president's helicopter travel,

599
00:41:32,700 --> 00:41:34,520
initially the task was shared with the Army.

600
00:41:35,180 --> 00:41:39,120
In fact, Nixon's memorable departure from the White House in 1974

601
00:41:39,121 --> 00:41:43,180
actually took place aboard the Army One helicopter.

602
00:41:45,440 --> 00:41:49,840
Like Air Force One, any Army or Marine vehicle transporting the President

603
00:41:49,841 --> 00:41:52,460
becomes Army One or Marine One.

604
00:41:54,140 --> 00:41:59,420
The Marine HMX-1 squadron took over sole responsibility in 1975.

605
00:42:00,420 --> 00:42:05,300
They bought additional new H3s.

606
00:42:05,400 --> 00:42:06,800
Those are still in service today.

607
00:42:06,801 --> 00:42:11,740
The differences are some technology improvements with the rotor head and so on,

608
00:42:12,200 --> 00:42:16,500
and vibration dampening, and power plants have improved.

609
00:42:16,660 --> 00:42:19,120
General Electric makes those power plants.

610
00:42:23,140 --> 00:42:29,400
The current Marine 1H3 can travel 140 miles per hour at 14,400 feet

611
00:42:29,401 --> 00:42:32,200
for up to 600 miles, depending on its payload.

612
00:42:32,201 --> 00:42:40,660
energy absorbent landing gear and crash resistant fuel tanks make it safer it employs light armor

613
00:42:40,661 --> 00:42:46,060
special protection from electronics damaging electromagnetic pulses and probably weighs about

614
00:42:46,061 --> 00:42:53,560
10 tons but most information about the craft is top secret but we do know that the four-person

615
00:42:53,561 --> 00:43:01,000
crew can serve 15 passengers sound dampeners air conditioning a galley and a restroom contribute to

616
00:43:01,001 --> 00:43:01,849
its comfort.

617
00:43:01,850 --> 00:43:07,720
It has two 1,500 horsepower general electric turboshaft engines, but it can stay in

618
00:43:07,721 --> 00:43:10,093
the air with only one functioning.

619
00:43:10,094 --> 00:43:14,280
It's the smoothest, I believe, I'm prejudiced, the smoothest

620
00:43:14,281 --> 00:43:18,461
helicopter ride you could have. Certainly the most comfortable and most quiet.

621
00:43:18,462 --> 00:43:19,640
The president also has

622
00:43:19,641 --> 00:43:25,500
a smaller, modified Sikorsky Blackhawk, which can resist an attack from 23 millimeter shells

623
00:43:25,501 --> 00:43:29,320
while traveling at speeds approaching 180 miles per hour.

624
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:34,540
Four crew members and eight passengers can travel up to 445 miles,

625
00:43:35,120 --> 00:43:36,880
carrying over 20,000 pounds.

626
00:43:37,700 --> 00:43:41,620
Two General Electric 1630 horsepower engines keep it aloft.

627
00:43:43,700 --> 00:43:47,160
Although Marine One offers the convenience of quick, short flights

628
00:43:47,161 --> 00:43:50,980
that avoid traffic and the logistical challenges of presidential ground transport,

629
00:43:50,981 --> 00:43:57,900
its greatest asset is its ability to remove the president from danger faster than any other presidential mover.

630
00:43:58,460 --> 00:44:05,160
Marine One would very quickly get to the president and move him away from that concern or threat

631
00:44:05,161 --> 00:44:09,700
or move him to a medical facility, God forbid, if that happened.

632
00:44:10,980 --> 00:44:14,840
Marine One, like all presidential movers, has its utilitarian purpose.

633
00:44:15,860 --> 00:44:18,540
Each presidential mover gets the president from here to there.

634
00:44:18,541 --> 00:44:21,540
But they are more than just vehicles.

635
00:44:21,541 --> 00:44:24,540
They help presidents make history.

636
00:44:24,541 --> 00:44:28,540
And occasionally, they make history themselves.

